DOCK-010|P0|A trustworthy running state is required for core app navigation.|DOCK-012/013; process/window identity model in M6.|Given an app state that is known to the tracker, when it transitions between open and closed, then its indicator follows that state; unknown state is not represented as a confirmed open/closed value.|M1 state review; Windows app/window tracking integration in M6; REF-04 Mac sample NOT TESTED.
DOCK-011|P1|Launch motion is fidelity feedback, not the launch mechanism itself.|DOCK-012; DOCK-034; animation/reduced-motion policy in M5/M10.|Given a launch request, when startup is pending, then any enabled launch motion is bounded and stops on success, failure, timeout, or reduced-motion policy; launch outcome remains correct with animation disabled.|M1 behavior review; Windows animation/launch integration in M5/M6; REF-03/04 Mac sample NOT TESTED.
DOCK-012|P0|Launching a selected app is the central interaction.|DOCK-005; Shell/package launch adapter and item identity in M6.|Given a valid pinned target, when activated once, then one launch request reaches that target; unsupported or failed launches return actionable feedback and do not silently invoke a different target.|M1 behavior review; Windows launch integration in M6; REF-04 Mac sample NOT TESTED.
DOCK-013|P0|Window activation and focus behavior are architectural requirements.|DOCK-010/012; HWND/app identity and Windows foreground policy in M6; M0 non-activation gate.|Given an app with an existing window, when its Dock item is activated, then the intended window is requested for activation without bypassing Windows foreground rules; refusal is distinguishable from launch failure and does not steal focus arbitrarily.|M1 behavior review; authorized Windows window integration in M6; REF-04 Mac sample NOT TESTED.
