DOCK-037|P0|Keyboard and assistive access is part of baseline interaction quality.|Object model; focus traversal; UI Automation names/roles/states; input host route from M0.|Given only keyboard and accessibility APIs, when a user traverses, opens, cancels, or requests a context action, then each core item is named, focus is visible, and action/state are reported without mouse injection.|M1 interaction/accessibility review; Windows keyboard/UIA integration in M10; REF-07 Mac sample NOT TESTED.
DOCK-038|P1|Badges provide app information but must have a trusted source.|DOCK-005; authorized notification or app provider; privacy settings.|Given an authorized provider reports a badge, when displayed, then its value and accessible description match that provider; without a provider or after permission withdrawal, no fabricated badge remains.|M1 provider/privacy review; Windows provider integration in M4/M10; REF-02 Mac sample NOT TESTED.
DOCK-039|P2|Handoff depends on Apple-specific cross-device services and is not Windows MVP scope.|User-approved cross-device provider, privacy/identity model, and explicit product decision.|Given no supported and authorized cross-device provider exists, when the Dock starts, then no empty or misleading Handoff slot appears; a provider is added only after separate security and feasibility review.|M1 scope review; defer implementation pending independent decision; REF-04 Mac sample NOT TESTED.
DOCK-040|P1|Monitor and virtual desktop behavior follows core layout but depends on physical topology.|DOCK-001/002/033; display topology and per-monitor DPI profile in M3/M9.|Given a display is added, removed, or selected as target, when topology changes, then Dock ownership follows the selected policy and settings survive reconfiguration without orphaned windows.|M1 policy review; Windows multi-monitor integration in M3/M9; REF-06 Mac sample NOT TESTED and Windows second display unavailable in current evidence.
DOCK-041|P0|Full-screen non-interference is a foundational system coexistence rule.|DOCK-013/033/046; window state detection and non-activation policy; M0 gate.|Given a maximized, borderless-fullscreen, or DXGI-exclusive app, when it enters/leaves that state, then the Dock does not steal focus or interfere with input/fullscreen and resumes the user's prior visibility policy; visibility over exclusive fullscreen is not required.|M1 state/acceptance review; authorized Windows window integration in M9 after M0 gate; REF-06 Mac sample NOT TESTED.
