DOCK-009|P1|Recent items add parity but require privacy policy and are not required for the first launch/navigation slice.|M2 recent-item projection and approved privacy preference; the display toggle shares that preference and is not a prerequisite to the recent-item model.|Given recent suggestions are enabled, when eligible apps are observed, then pinned apps are excluded, identities are deduplicated, no more than three unpinned recent apps are shown, and disabling the feature stops display without disturbing pins.
DOCK-013|P0|Window activation and focus behavior are architectural requirements.|M6 HWND/app identity and Windows foreground policy; M0 non-activation gate; indicator and launch animation are not prerequisites.|Given an app has an eligible existing window, when its Dock item is activated, then the Dock requests that exact window; if Windows refuses foreground activation, the current foreground remains unchanged and the outcome is distinguishable from launch failure.
DOCK-015|P1|Context menus broaden common item actions after core launch/navigation.|Per-item menu command model, keyboard/UIA route, and capability checks for each advertised command.|Given an item and its current state, when its context menu is requested, then only supported commands appear, each is named and invokable, and high-impact commands also have an accessible alternate route.
DOCK-017|P2|Option-click parity is optional and can conflict with Windows input conventions.|User-approved mapping and safe window actions; shared app/window identity; DOCK-013 activation command is a consumer, not a prerequisite.|Given an explicitly enabled Option-click equivalent and a previous eligible app, when invoked on the current app, then the previous app is requested for activation and only the current app is hidden; disabling the mapping restores ordinary click behavior.
DOCK-018|P2|Hiding other apps is a high-impact parity action and should be opt-in.|Explicit user scope and safe window policy; shared app/window identity; no elevated or cross-integrity operation.|Given an explicitly enabled action and a selected eligible app, when invoked, then that app is requested for activation and only eligible peer app windows are hidden; cancellation or policy denial leaves all unrelated windows unchanged.
DOCK-019|P1|Locating an item helps users recover its source and diagnose broken links.|Persistent target identity/path and Explorer integration in M6/M8.|Given a target whose path still exists, when Locate is selected, then Explorer selects the corresponding target; if it is missing or inaccessible, the Dock reports that state and offers a repair path.
DOCK-021|P0|A Windows file-manager role is a useful first-run mapping, but its default pin state needs owner approval.|App/item model and Windows Shell icon/launch provider; owner decision D-01 before choosing a default pinned item.|Given an owner-approved first-run file-manager mapping, when activated, then the configured Windows file manager opens with its traceable Windows icon; no name or asset claims to be Finder.
DOCK-022|P1|File/folder objects need a stable semantic zone distinct from apps.|DOCK-001; object-type and section model in M2.|Given app and file/folder entries, when the Dock is laid out at any supported edge, then object zones and their order remain explicit and app reordering cannot silently move a file into the app zone.
DOCK-024|P1|Folder and Stack representations are useful but must preserve the underlying directory.|Folder item model and view-state persistence in M8.|Given a folder reference, when it is shown as Folder or expanded as Stack, then only the Dock presentation changes; clicking the open Stack or desktop collapses it without changing directory contents, and Escape provides an accessible close path.
DOCK-025|P1|All three source-named Stack views should be implemented or explicitly carried as unmet.|DOCK-024 folder/Stack model; accessible popup and work-area layout in M8.|Given a Stack and each requested view mode in turn, when Fan, Grid, or List is selected, then every eligible item is reachable and selectable within the work area; a missing view is recorded as FAIL/deferred, never passed by substituting another mode.
DOCK-027|P1|Recycle Bin state is useful but can follow the first app-navigation slice; correctness is mandatory when the feature ships.|Windows Shell Recycle Bin state provider and icon source.|Given an empty, non-empty, or unqueryable Recycle Bin, when its icon state is rendered, then the state is accurate or explicitly unknown; query failure never fabricates empty/full.
DOCK-028|P1|Opening and clearing the Recycle Bin can follow the basic Dock slice; destructive clearing remains a feature-release gate.|Windows Recycle Bin command adapter and confirmation policy; shared state provider, not DOCK-027 presentation.|Given a non-empty Recycle Bin, when the user opens it or requests Empty, then Open launches the system view while Empty presents the approved confirmation; cancel preserves contents and confirmed success refreshes state.
DOCK-029|P1|File-to-Recycle-Bin integration is a later feature slice; no unsafe fallback is ever acceptable.|Supported Windows Shell recycle operation, volume/permission/error handling; shared state provider, not DOCK-027 presentation.|Given a supported file is dropped onto the Trash role, when the Shell recycle operation succeeds, then the file becomes recoverable; if unsupported or denied, the source remains intact and the Dock never falls back to permanent deletion.
DOCK-030|P1|Minimized-window representation depends on stable HWND ownership and a user preference.|M6 window identity/lifecycle model and preference persistence.|Given a tracked window and the app-icon mode is enabled, when it is minimized, then its app item represents the window; toggling modes does not lose the HWND association.
DOCK-031|P1|Standalone minimized proxies are the alternate mode to DOCK-030, not a dependency of it.|M6 window identity/lifecycle model and explicit representation-mode preference.|Given a tracked window and standalone-proxy mode is enabled, when it is minimized, then one proxy refers to that HWND and activation restores that same window; close removes only that proxy.
DOCK-032|P2|Custom minimize animation is polish and must not imply control of third-party renderers.|OS minimize/restore state source; renderer and reduced-motion policy in M5; A15 lists Genie/Scale setting names only.|Given a supported Dock-owned minimize/restore transition, when animation is enabled, then the visual animation completes or cancels without changing the OS window outcome; unsupported third-party animation interception is not claimed.
DOCK-033|P0|Single-display reachability and recovery matter before multi-display policy.|DOCK-001 single-display geometry; edge detection and visibility state machine in M3/M9; fullscreen suppression is a parallel safety contract, not a prerequisite.|Given auto-hide is enabled on one display, when the pointer reaches the configured edge, then the Dock reveals and later hides per the configured state; menus, drag, keyboard focus, and suppression states cannot strand the Dock hidden.
DOCK-034|P1|A separate animation control supports user choice and reduced motion.|M5/M10 animation preference and settings persistence; this preference can be implemented before the animation itself.|Given opening animation is disabled or reduced motion is requested, when an app launches, then the app still starts and its state updates without the opening animation.
DOCK-040|P1|Multi-display and virtual-desktop policy requires physical topology support beyond single-display layout.|DOCK-001/002 geometry; display topology and per-monitor DPI in M3/M9; auto-hide DOCK-033 is not a prerequisite.|Given a display is added, removed, or selected as Dock owner, when topology changes, then the Dock relocates or recovers according to the owner-approved policy and no orphaned Dock window remains.
DOCK-041|P0|Fullscreen non-interference is a system coexistence gate, not a requirement to overlay exclusive fullscreen.|M0 window-state and non-activation mechanism; Windows foreground policy; explicit classification of maximized, borderless, and DXGI-exclusive states.|Given a regular maximized or borderless-fullscreen app, when visibility policy changes, then focus and input remain correct; given DXGI-exclusive fullscreen, when the Dock starts or is running, then it neither forces mode exit nor intercepts input; after exit, the previous Dock policy recovers.
DOCK-043|P2|Force Quit is destructive and belongs behind explicit user intent.|Context command framework; process identity; explicit confirmation and unsaved-data warning.|Given an unresponsive eligible app, when Force Quit is selected, then the action clearly identifies its target and warns about data loss before termination; cancellation leaves the process untouched.
DOCK-042|P1|System-following appearance is a later visual theme input; custom Dock themes remain a product choice.|M4/M10 theme tokens and contrast policy; A16 only documents macOS appearance modes, not a Dock-specific setting; owner decision D-08.|Given the approved light/dark/follow-system policy, when the Windows theme changes, then Dock content remains legible and updates per that policy; no Apple visual match is claimed without reference samples.
DOCK-046|P0|No persistent obstruction and recoverability are baseline shell coexistence requirements.|DOCK-001 single-display geometry; window host and recovery behavior in M3/M9; taskbar coexistence and visibility policy.|Given normal desktop, maximized, borderless-fullscreen, or restored states, when Dock visibility changes, then taskbar and application work remain reachable, focus is not stolen, and the Dock recovers without manual restart.
'@ | ConvertFrom-Csv -Delimiter '|'

foreach ($fix in $correctionText) {
    $row = $matrix | Where-Object ID -eq $fix.ID
    if ($null -eq $row) { throw "Missing correction target $($fix.ID)" }
    $row.PriorityProposal = $fix.Priority
    $row.PriorityBasis = $fix.Basis
    $row.Dependency = $fix.Dependency
    $row.AcceptanceCheck = $fix.Acceptance
}

$ownerDecisionLinks = @{
    'DOCK-001' = 'D-03';
    'DOCK-006' = 'D-04'; 'DOCK-007' = 'D-04'; 'DOCK-008' = 'D-04';
    'DOCK-009' = 'D-02'; 'DOCK-013' = 'D-05'; 'DOCK-017' = 'D-05;D-07'; 'DOCK-018' = 'D-05;D-07';
    'DOCK-021' = 'D-01'; 'DOCK-022' = 'D-04'; 'DOCK-023' = 'D-04'; 'DOCK-024' = 'D-04';
    'DOCK-025' = 'D-04'; 'DOCK-026' = 'D-04'; 'DOCK-027' = 'D-04'; 'DOCK-028' = 'D-04;D-06';
    'DOCK-029' = 'D-04;D-06'; 'DOCK-033' = 'D-03;D-05'; 'DOCK-036' = 'D-02;D-04';
    'DOCK-037' = 'D-05'; 'DOCK-041' = 'D-03;D-05'; 'DOCK-042' = 'D-08'; 'DOCK-046' = 'D-03;D-05'
}

$decisionSeedText = @'
ID|Seed|Acceptance
DOCK-001|Windows product decision D-03 sets Bottom as the initial edge; Left and Right remain user selectable. Never change taskbar settings automatically. Exact geometry and collision handling remain unverified.|Given a fresh profile, when the Dock selects its initial edge, then Bottom is selected; changing to Left or Right is user initiated and does not mutate Windows taskbar settings.
DOCK-006|D-04 permits user customization of semantic zones; adding an entry creates a Dock reference only.|Given a valid application target, when it is pinned, then a Dock reference is added and the target installation and files remain unchanged.
DOCK-007|D-04 requires removing only the Dock reference; never delete or uninstall the target.|Given a pinned entry, when it is removed, then the Dock reference disappears while its target remains unchanged and available.
DOCK-009|D-02 enables Recent by default, caps it at three unpinned apps, and permits only minimal local data with no upload. A missing reliable activity source means the list stays empty.|Given no reliable local activity observation exists, when Recent is projected, then no entries are fabricated; given valid observations, then no more than three unpinned targets appear and clearing removes only local Recent state.
DOCK-013|D-05 prioritizes Windows foreground rules and maintainability; no focus-stealing workaround is permitted.|Given an app has an eligible existing window, when activation is requested, then Core emits an intent and the adapter reports foreground denial distinctly without bypassing Windows policy.
DOCK-017|D-07 says macOS modifier mappings are not enabled by default; advanced configurable bindings may be added later.|Given a fresh profile, when input mappings are loaded, then no Option/Command compatibility mapping is active.
DOCK-018|D-05/D-07 require safe, explicit, configurable scope; no modifier mapping or cross-integrity action is enabled by default.|Given a fresh profile, when input mappings are loaded, then hide-other-apps is unavailable until explicitly configured and approved by the user.
DOCK-021|D-01 sets Explorer as the default Windows file-manager role, not as Finder or an Apple asset.|Given a fresh Core state, when defaults are created, then one Windows file-manager role exists in the fixed section; its actual Shell launch remains unimplemented.
DOCK-022|D-04 preserves fixed app, recent app, and files/folders semantic sections while allowing user-controlled references and ordering.|Given entries from different semantic sections, when one section is reordered, then entries in other sections stay in place and targets are never moved or deleted.
DOCK-028|D-06 requires explicit confirmation for Empty in the initial release, with no default bypass option.|Given the Empty Recycle Bin command is requested, when confirmation is declined, then no destructive Shell command is issued; confirming is a separate adapter action.
DOCK-029|D-04/D-06 prohibit permanent-delete fallback for failed recycle operations; removal of a Dock reference remains separate from file deletion.|Given a recycle operation is unsupported or denied, when the adapter reports failure, then Core retains the source target and does not issue permanent deletion.
DOCK-033|D-03 prohibits changing taskbar settings to make room; users can choose the Dock edge and hide policy.|Given a bottom-edge collision is detected, when compatibility is presented, then only Dock settings or user guidance are offered and no taskbar setting is changed.
DOCK-036|D-02 sets Recent enabled by default, local-only, clearable and disableable; absence of an activity provider yields an empty projection.|Given the user disables or clears Recent, when state is persisted, then the setting is respected and fixed items/order remain intact; no usage data is uploaded.
DOCK-041|D-05 requires Windows-correct fullscreen coexistence and forbids unstable focus or input bypasses.|Given DXGI exclusive fullscreen is active, when a Dock command or visibility policy is processed, then Core makes no mode/focus manipulation request and can represent adapter denial/suppression.
DOCK-042|D-08 sets FollowSystem as the initial Windows appearance policy and permits an independent Light/Dark override; material values remain unmeasured.|Given a fresh profile, when theme preferences are read, then FollowSystem is selected; setting Light or Dark changes only Dock preference and claims no Mac visual match.
DOCK-046|D-03 sets Bottom as the default while leaving taskbar settings untouched; collision handling must provide user-controlled Dock choices.|Given a bottom-edge space conflict, when the compatibility path is shown, then the Dock remains recoverable, the taskbar stays unchanged, and the user may select another Dock edge.
'@ | ConvertFrom-Csv -Delimiter '|'

$decisionSeeds = @{}
foreach ($decision in $decisionSeedText) { $decisionSeeds[$decision.ID] = $decision }
foreach ($row in $matrix) {
    $refs = @('D-09')
    if ($ownerDecisionLinks.ContainsKey($row.ID)) { $refs += $ownerDecisionLinks[$row.ID].Split(';') }
    $row | Add-Member -Force -NotePropertyName OwnerDecisionRefs -NotePropertyValue (($refs | Sort-Object -Unique) -join ';')
    if ($decisionSeeds.ContainsKey($row.ID)) {
        $row.ProposedProductBehaviorAndAcceptanceSeed = $decisionSeeds[$row.ID].Seed
        if (-not [string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($decisionSeeds[$row.ID].Acceptance)) {
            $row.AcceptanceCheck = $decisionSeeds[$row.ID].Acceptance
        }
    }
}

# These entries have a tested Core-only slice. The full Dock requirement remains unaccepted.
$coreSliceStatuses = @{
    'DOCK-001' = 'PARTIAL - CORE PREFERENCE ONLY'
    'DOCK-006' = 'PARTIAL - CORE ITEM STATE ONLY'
    'DOCK-007' = 'PARTIAL - CORE REFERENCE REMOVAL ONLY'
    'DOCK-008' = 'PARTIAL - CORE ORDER AND CODEC ONLY'
    'DOCK-009' = 'PARTIAL - CORE RECENT STATE ONLY'
    'DOCK-012' = 'PARTIAL - COMMAND CONTRACT ONLY'
    'DOCK-013' = 'PARTIAL - COMMAND CONTRACT ONLY'
    'DOCK-021' = 'PARTIAL - DEFAULT ROLE MODEL ONLY'
    'DOCK-022' = 'PARTIAL - CORE SECTIONS AND ORDER ONLY'
    'DOCK-036' = 'PARTIAL - CORE RECENT PREFERENCE ONLY'
    'DOCK-042' = 'PARTIAL - APPEARANCE PREFERENCE ONLY'
}
foreach ($row in $matrix) {
    $row.ImplementationStatus = if ($coreSliceStatuses.ContainsKey($row.ID)) {
        $coreSliceStatuses[$row.ID]
    } else {
        'NOT STARTED'
    }
}

foreach ($row in $matrix) {
    $audit = $auditById[$row.ID]
    if ($null -eq $audit) { throw "Missing source review for $($row.ID)" }
    $row.OfficialSourceStatus = $audit.Status
    $row | Add-Member -Force -NotePropertyName EvidenceScopeReview -NotePropertyValue $audit.Review
}

$matrix | Where-Object ID -eq 'DOCK-032' | ForEach-Object { $_.OfficialAppleReferences = 'A2;A15' }
$matrix | Where-Object ID -eq 'DOCK-042' | ForEach-Object { $_.OfficialAppleReferences = 'A2;A16' }

$matrix | Export-Csv -LiteralPath $matrixPath -NoTypeInformation -Encoding utf8
Write-Output "Updated $($matrix.Count) provisional requirements in $matrixPath"
